---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Mapathon: All About Maps | tcplot: Data Visualization in Minneapolis and St. Paul"
    description="Bring a map you love, then build one together — from MapLibre and QGIS to hand-drawn cartography — and present what you made."
>
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8">
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-8 mb-12">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">
                Upcoming: Mapathon — All About Maps
            </h2>

            <div class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
                <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">
                    Saturday, Oct 24 · 10:00 AM to 12:00 PM CDT
                </p>
                <p class="flex items-start gap-2 mt-1">
                    <svg
                        class="w-5 h-5 mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-gray-400"
                        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                        fill="currentColor"
                        aria-hidden="true"
                    >
                        <path
                            fill-rule="evenodd"
                            d="M12 2c-3.866 0-7 3.07-7 6.857 0 4.34 4.686 9.86 6.412 11.74a.8.8 0 0 0 1.176 0C14.314 18.717 19 13.197 19 8.857 19 5.07 15.866 2 12 2Zm0 9.5a2.5 2.5 0 1 1 0-5 2.5 2.5 0 0 1 0 5Z"
                            clip-rule="evenodd"></path>
                    </svg>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >The Coffee Shop NE</span
                        ><br />2852 Johnson St NE · Minneapolis, MN</span
                    >
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Every map is a chart with a place in it.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Join <span class="font-silkscreen">tcplot</span> for a morning devoted
                entirely to maps. First, everyone brings one map they love and gives
                it a two-minute show-and-tell; then we open the laptops and build
                maps of our own, ending with a round of presentations.
            </p>

            <div class="flex justify-center mt-12 mb-8">
                <div class="max-w-2xl w-full">
                    <div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
                        <img
                            src="/images/meiji-japan-prefecture-map-1870s.jpg"
                            alt="A Meiji-era outline map of Japan with prefectures hand-tinted in soft green, orange, yellow, and purple"
                            class="w-full h-auto object-contain"
                        />
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mt-2">
                        A Meiji-era outline map of Japan (大日本暗射地図,
                        1870s), the newly drawn prefectures hand-tinted in
                        watercolor.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Act one: bring a map
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Your ticket in is one map, any map, and two minutes on why it's
                great or terrible. For example:
            </p>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-8">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >A classic — Minard, Snow's cholera map, a du Bois
                        plate, a favorite USGS quad</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >Something interactive you keep going back to — a
                        transit map, a wind map, a snow-depth map you check
                        every winter</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >A paper map with a history — the state park map from
                        the glovebox, a hand-me-down atlas page</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >Something you made yourself, at any level of finish</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >A bad map you love to hate — misleading choropleth,
                        crime against projections, 3D pie chart on a globe</span
                    >
                </li>
            </ul>

            <div class="flex justify-center mt-12 mb-8">
                <div class="max-w-2xl w-full">
                    <div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
                        <img
                            src="/images/mercator-hondius-1606-north-pole.jpg"
                            alt="Gerardus Mercator's 1606 map of the Arctic, showing an invented polar continent divided by four rivers, ringed by ornate gold scrollwork"
                            class="w-full h-auto object-contain"
                        />
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mt-2">
                        Gerardus Mercator's <em
                            >Septentrionalium Terrarum descriptio</em
                        > (1606 Hondius edition), the first printed map devoted to
                        the North Pole. The polar continent, its four rivers, and
                        the magnetic mountain at the center are entirely invented
                        — a masterpiece of confident wrongness, and the patron saint
                        of "bring a bad map you love."
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Act two: build together
            </h3>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-8">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Tooling primer.</span
                        > A short walk through the modern mapping stack and the local
                        data sources below, so nobody spends the build hour googling
                        "how to make a map."</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Build session.</span
                        > Solo or in small groups, make a map — of your neighborhood,
                        your commute, the Twin Cities, anywhere. Code, GIS, or pen
                        and paper all count. AI assistants welcome. They're good at
                        GeoJSON plumbing.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Present.</span
                        > Show what you made, however far you got.</span
                    >
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                The mapping stack, briefly
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Pick a lane based on how you like to work.
            </p>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-8">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Fast and no-code:</span
                        > Datawrapper and Felt make a clean choropleth or pin map
                        in minutes; kepler.gl eats a CSV of points and gives you something
                        gorgeous back.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Web code:</span
                        > Leaflet for the friendly classic, MapLibre GL for vector
                        tiles and smooth zooms, deck.gl when you have a hundred thousand
                        points, D3 when you want full control of the projection.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Analysis-first:</span
                        > QGIS (free, deep, desktop) or Python's GeoPandas + Folium
                        if your map starts life as a dataframe.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900">Analog:</span
                        > Hand-drawn cartography is a real and honorable lane — the
                        show-and-tell round will prove it.</span
                    >
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Local data to map
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                The Twin Cities are unusually rich in open geodata. A starter
                list:
            </p>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-8">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><a
                            href="https://opendata.minneapolismn.gov"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >Minneapolis Open Data</a
                        > — the city tree inventory, bike lanes, 311 calls, scooter
                        trips, and much more</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><a
                            href="https://gisdata.mn.gov"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >Minnesota Geospatial Commons</a
                        > — statewide layers: lakes, land cover, parcels, LiDAR</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >Metro Transit's GTFS feed — every route, stop, and
                        schedule in the metro, ready to draw</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >OpenStreetMap via the Overpass API — query every coffee
                        shop, little free library, or water fountain in the
                        metro</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        >Your own GPS traces — running routes, bike rides, the
                        year of walks in your exercise app</span
                    >
                </li>
            </ul>

            <div class="flex justify-center mt-12 mb-8">
                <div class="max-w-2xl w-full">
                    <div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
                        <img
                            src="/images/darton-1823-mountains-and-rivers.jpg"
                            alt="An 1823 comparative chart showing the world's principal rivers uncoiled and hanging side by side, beside the principal mountains stacked against a shared height scale"
                            class="w-full h-auto object-contain"
                        />
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mt-2">
                        W. R. Gardner's <em
                            >Comparative Heights of the Principal Mountains and
                            Lengths of the Principal Rivers in the World</em
                        > (William Darton, London, 1823). The rivers are uncoiled
                        and hung side by side; the mountains queue up against one
                        shared scale. Not quite a map, not quite a chart — evidence
                        that the border between the two has always been open.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Two cartography rules worth knowing going in
            </h3>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-8">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Normalize your choropleths.</span
                        > A map of raw counts is usually just a population map wearing
                        a costume. Divide by area or population before you color anything
                        in.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Projections are a choice, not a default.</span
                        > Every flat map of a round world distorts something — area,
                        shape, or distance — and Web Mercator quietly chooses for
                        you. At city scale you can mostly ignore it; at any larger
                        scale, choosing is part of the design.</span
                    >
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                What to bring
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                One map you love (on a phone, a laptop, or paper) and, if you
                want to build digitally, a laptop. No GIS experience needed —
                the tooling primer starts from zero, and paper and markers will
                be on hand for the analog lane.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Who this is for
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Anyone who has ever stared at a map longer than they needed to.
                GIS professionals, web developers, runners with GPS archives,
                and people who just really like atlases will all find their
                lane.
            </p>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                Hope to see you there!
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</Layout>
